==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*sent- (feel)}}
From {{bor|en|la|sentiēns||feeling, perceiving}}, present active participle of {{m|la|sentiō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɛn.ti.ənt/}}<ref name=Cambridge>{{cite-web|url=https://dictionary.cambridge.org/pronunciation/english/sentient|title=SENTIENT {{!}} Pronunciation in English|publisher=Cambridge University Press|accessdate=2020-09-18}}</ref>
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-sentient.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɛn.ʃ(i.)ənt/}}<ref name=Cambridge/>

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Experiencing [[sensation]], [[thought]], or [[feeling]].
#: {{syn|en|sensate}}
# Able to consciously [[perceive]] through the use of [[sense]] faculties.
#: {{ant|en|insensate}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|science fiction}} Possessing human-like awareness and intelligence.
#: {{syn|en|sapient|Thesaurus:self-aware}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/jun/14/labelling-googles-lamda-chatbot-as-sentient-is-fanciful-but-its-very-human-to-be-taken-in-by-machines|title=Labelling Google’s LaMDA chatbot as sentient is fanciful. But it’s very human to be taken in by machines|author=Toby Walsh|authorlink=Toby Walsh|date=2022-06-14|work=The Guardian|passage=Lemoine has been placed on “paid administrative leave” after publishing a transcript of conversations with LaMDA which he claims support his belief that the chatbot is '''sentient''' and comparable to a seven- or eight-year-old child. {{...}} While Lemoine no doubt genuinely believes his claims, LaMDA is likely to be as '''sentient''' as a traffic light.}}

